Objective
The objective of this lab was to identify and exploit improper group permissions within a Linux environment, specifically focusing on the Docker group. The goal was to understand how membership in privileged groups can lead to privilege escalation and full system compromise.
Step 1: Enumerating User Groups
I began by identifying the groups assigned to the current user.
During this process, I discovered:
The user was a member of the Docker group
Elevated privileges associated with Docker group membership
This highlighted a common misconfiguration where users are granted excessive permissions through group assignments.
Step 2: Understanding Docker Group Privileges
Next, I analyzed the implications of being part of the Docker group.
Through this, I observed:
Docker allows interaction with containers as root
Users in the Docker group can effectively gain root-level access
This demonstrated how Docker group membership can be abused to bypass normal privilege restrictions.
Step 3: Exploiting Docker for Privilege Escalation
Using Docker, I leveraged my group membership to escalate privileges.
This allowed:
Running a container with elevated permissions
Accessing the host system with root privileges
This step showed how attackers can use Docker to break out of restricted environments and gain full control of the system.
Step 4: Gaining Elevated Access
After exploiting Docker, I successfully escalated privileges.
This resulted in:
Root-level access on the system
Full control over system resources
This confirmed that Docker group misconfiguration can directly lead to complete system compromise.
Step 5: Impact Analysis
Combining all findings, the system was vulnerable due to improper group permissions:
User membership in the Docker group
Lack of restrictions on Docker usage
Ability to execute privileged containers
An attacker could leverage these issues to:
Escalate privileges to root
Access and modify system files
Maintain persistent access
Conclusion & Outcome
Through this lab, I gained hands-on experience exploiting Docker group permissions for privilege escalation. This exercise reinforced the importance of restricting access to privileged groups and properly securing container environments.
It also highlighted how seemingly minor permission issues can lead to full system compromise.
